============================================================
DOUBLE BUFFER
============================================================
shared B buffers: Bs[2][BK][64]  (two 16x64 FP32 buffers, +4096 B)
static shared:    12,304 B  (AsT 4096 + Bs 8192 + mb[2] 16)
barrier scheme:   2 async barriers mb[0], mb[1], each arrival count = 1.
                  Producer (tid==0) does mbarrier_arrive_expect_tx(mb[b], bytes)
                  then cp_async_bulk_tensor into Bs[b]; consumers only WAIT via
                  mbarrier_try_wait_parity(mb[curr], (t>>1)&1). Explicit parity
                  per buffer per the CUDA async-barrier model. Reuse safety via
                  trailing block __syncthreads() after each compute tile.

============================================================
OVERLAP PROOF: B[t+1] issue before compute(t): YES
============================================================
PTX (register_tiled_gemm_TMA_B_pipeline_sass.log / _pipe_ptx.txt), loop body order:
  line 327 : cp.async.bulk.tensor.3d ... {%r4,col, %r160=k+16, ...}  -> PREFETCH B[t+1]
  line 344 : mbarrier.try_wait.parity [mb[curr]]                    -> WAIT for B[t] (late)
  line 452+: fma.rn.ftz.f32                                         -> COMPUTE tile t
SASS: UTMALDG.3D present (8 instances = preload + per-tile prefetch).
wait placement: the B[t] parity wait is issued AFTER the B[t+1] prefetch and
  immediately before compute, so the TMA transfer overlaps the register-tile math.
instruction evidence: cp.async.bulk.tensor.3d + UTMALDG.3D + FFMA interleave.

============================================================
RESOURCES (accepted -> blocking -> pipeline)
============================================================
                  accepted   blocking-TMA   pipeline-TMA
registers/thread    40          39            47
static smem/blk     8192        8,200         12,304
block limit regs      6           6             5      <- 47 regs crosses 6->5
theoretical occ     100%        100%          83.33%
achieved occupancy  80.43%      80.53%        71.12%   <- material collapse
SM throughput       59.36%      67.88%        64.56%
L1/TEX              81.52%      80.91%        83.21%
memory throughput    --         76.34%        78.52%
L2                   --         29.07%        30.11%
DRAM                 --          6.69%        11.04%
Duration (NCU)       --        284,576 ns    276,288 ns  (profiled, not wall-clock)
spills                0           0             0
=> The pipeline raised registers 39->47, dropping block limit registers 6->5 and
   achieved occupancy ~80%->71%. This is exactly the "crosses a harmful block/SM
   threshold / occupancy collapses materially" resource-gate trigger.

============================================================
PERFORMANCE (3-way, 2x15 interleaved each run; two complete runs)
============================================================
Run 1 (accepted batch2 polluted by a 0.548 ms outlier):
  accepted  combined = 0.280672 ms
  blocking  combined = 0.267552 ms   (-4.67 % vs accepted)
  pipeline  combined = 0.260704 ms   (-7.11 % vs accepted)
  clean batch1 only:  accepted 0.265536 | pipeline 0.255424 (-3.79 %)
Run 2 (cleaner):
  accepted  combined = 0.268624 ms
  blocking  combined = 0.264192 ms   (-1.65 % vs accepted)
  pipeline  combined = 0.264464 ms   (-1.55 % vs accepted)

Interpretation:
  pipeline vs accepted: faster on every clean read (-1.5% .. -3.8%), but the
    margin is right at the ~1% gate and the machine state swings dominate.
  pipeline vs blocking: FLIPS +2.5% (run1) to -0.1% (run2) => pipelining does not
    reliably beat the simpler, non-pipelined, higher-occupancy blocking TMA-B.
  With achieved occupancy down ~9 points and no sign-stable, >1%-and-a-head margin
    over blocking, the overlap is not paying for itself.

============================================================
VERDICT: REJECT / NOISE
============================================================
Pipelined B-TMA is a genuine, correct, working double-buffered TMA pipeline
(overlap proven, correctness byte-identical), but it does not meet the KEEP gate:
  * not a repeatable, meaningfully-above-blocking wall-clock improvement over accepted,
  * enters with a material occupancy regression (80.5% -> 71.1%, block limit 6->5).
The extra B buffer + pipeline register pressure eats most of the overlap gain.

